1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,021
In the summer of 751,

2
00:00:02,021 --> 00:00:04,683
two of the largest empires on Earth

3
00:00:04,683 --> 00:00:07,097
met for the first and only time

4
00:00:07,097 --> 00:00:07,902
in battle.

5
00:00:08,122 --> 00:00:09,769
On one side, Tang China,

6
00:00:09,769 --> 00:00:11,855
the richest state in the world.

7
00:00:12,075 --> 00:00:14,984
On the other, a brand-new Islamic dynasty,

8
00:00:14,984 --> 00:00:15,915
the Abbasids.

9
00:00:16,385 --> 00:00:18,281
They fought for five days by a

10
00:00:18,281 --> 00:00:19,628
river in Central Asia.

11
00:00:19,848 --> 00:00:20,972
On the fifth day,

12
00:00:20,972 --> 00:00:23,475
a third of the battlefield changed sides.

13
00:00:23,695 --> 00:00:25,472
This is the Battle of Talas,

14
00:00:25,472 --> 00:00:28,061
and almost everything you've heard about it

15
00:00:28,061 --> 00:00:30,244
is more complicated than the legend.

16
00:00:30,464 --> 00:00:32,545
If you like stories that check the

17
00:00:32,545 --> 00:00:34,726
facts, hit like, it really helps the

18
00:00:34,726 --> 00:00:35,221
channel.

19
00:00:35,691 --> 00:00:36,843
Look at the map.

20
00:00:37,063 --> 00:00:39,554
Here is Chang'an, the Tang capital,

21
00:00:39,554 --> 00:00:40,562
today's Xi'an.

22
00:00:40,782 --> 00:00:43,415
Here is Baghdad, which the Abbasids would

23
00:00:43,415 --> 00:00:45,027
found eleven years later.

24
00:00:45,497 --> 00:00:48,813
Between them, three thousand miles of desert,

25
00:00:48,813 --> 00:00:50,878
mountains, and oasis cities:

26
00:00:50,878 --> 00:00:52,943
Samarkand, Bukhara, Tashkent.

27
00:00:53,163 --> 00:00:56,061
Whoever controlled these cities controlled the Silk

28
00:00:56,061 --> 00:00:56,405
Road.

29
00:00:56,875 --> 00:00:58,771
The battle took place here,

30
00:00:58,771 --> 00:01:00,609
in the Talas River valley,

31
00:01:00,609 --> 00:01:02,734
near the modern city of Taraz,

32
00:01:02,734 --> 00:01:05,835
right on today's border between Kazakhstan and

33
00:01:05,835 --> 00:01:06,582
Kyrgyzstan.

34
00:01:06,802 --> 00:01:09,063
The exact field has never been found.

35
00:01:09,533 --> 00:01:11,994
By the 740s the Tang dynasty had

36
00:01:11,994 --> 00:01:15,070
pushed further west than any Chinese state

37
00:01:15,070 --> 00:01:15,869
before it.

38
00:01:16,089 --> 00:01:18,359
Its best general in the west was

39
00:01:18,359 --> 00:01:21,252
Gao Xianzhi, a commander of Korean descent.

40
00:01:21,722 --> 00:01:23,740
He had led an army across the

41
00:01:23,740 --> 00:01:26,413
Pamir mountains into what is now northern

42
00:01:26,413 --> 00:01:27,013
Pakistan.

43
00:01:27,233 --> 00:01:29,429
That march is still studied as one

44
00:01:29,429 --> 00:01:32,097
of the great mountain campaigns in history.

45
00:01:32,567 --> 00:01:34,126
Meanwhile in the west,

46
00:01:34,126 --> 00:01:37,303
the Umayyad Caliphate had just been overthrown.

47
00:01:37,523 --> 00:01:40,662
In 750 a revolution that started in

48
00:01:40,662 --> 00:01:44,604
Khorasan, eastern Iran, put the Abbasid family

49
00:01:44,604 --> 00:01:45,480
in power.

50
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:48,074
Their strongman in the east was Abu

51
00:01:48,074 --> 00:01:50,614
Muslim, the man who had organised that

52
00:01:50,614 --> 00:01:51,332
revolution.

53
00:01:51,802 --> 00:01:54,106
The war started with a local quarrel.

54
00:01:54,326 --> 00:01:57,588
In 750 Gao Xianzhi attacked the kingdom

55
00:01:57,588 --> 00:01:59,809
of Shash, today's Tashkent.

56
00:02:00,029 --> 00:02:02,747
Its ruler was taken to Chang'an and

57
00:02:02,747 --> 00:02:03,442
executed.

58
00:02:03,912 --> 00:02:06,450
His son escaped and asked the Abbasids

59
00:02:06,450 --> 00:02:07,112
for help.

60
00:02:07,332 --> 00:02:09,648
Abu Muslim saw a chance and sent

61
00:02:09,648 --> 00:02:11,791
an army under Ziyad ibn Salih.

62
00:02:12,011 --> 00:02:14,592
Gao decided to strike first and marched

63
00:02:14,592 --> 00:02:14,976
west.

64
00:02:15,446 --> 00:02:17,473
Here's where you have to be careful.

65
00:02:17,693 --> 00:02:21,372
Arab writers said the Chinese brought 100,000

66
00:02:21,372 --> 00:02:21,789
men.

67
00:02:22,009 --> 00:02:24,583
Chinese records say about 30,000:

68
00:02:24,583 --> 00:02:27,619
perhaps 10,000 to 20,000 Tang soldiers,

69
00:02:27,619 --> 00:02:29,732
and the rest Karluk Turks,

70
00:02:29,732 --> 00:02:32,702
nomads from the steppe who fought for

71
00:02:32,702 --> 00:02:33,230
China.

72
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:37,180
Most modern historians think something like 30,000

73
00:02:37,180 --> 00:02:39,460
on each side is more realistic.

74
00:02:39,680 --> 00:02:42,733
Medieval numbers are almost always inflated,

75
00:02:42,733 --> 00:02:45,547
so treat any big figure with suspicion.

76
00:02:45,767 --> 00:02:47,601
That is true for both sides.

77
00:02:48,071 --> 00:02:50,106
The armies met by the Talas River

78
00:02:50,106 --> 00:02:52,339
and fought for several days without a

79
00:02:52,339 --> 00:02:52,786
winner.

80
00:02:53,006 --> 00:02:55,560
Tang crossbowmen were feared across Asia,

81
00:02:55,560 --> 00:02:57,635
and Gao's men held their ground.

82
00:02:58,105 --> 00:02:59,435
Then, on the fifth day,

83
00:02:59,435 --> 00:03:01,040
some accounts say the fourth,

84
00:03:01,040 --> 00:03:02,095
the Karluks turned.

85
00:03:02,315 --> 00:03:04,620
They attacked the Tang army from behind

86
00:03:04,620 --> 00:03:07,072
while the Abbasids pressed from the front.

87
00:03:07,292 --> 00:03:09,726
The Karluks were around two-thirds of Gao's

88
00:03:09,726 --> 00:03:10,108
force.

89
00:03:10,578 --> 00:03:12,925
Chinese chronicles call it treachery.

90
00:03:13,145 --> 00:03:15,814
Some Arab historians say the Karluks had

91
00:03:15,814 --> 00:03:17,817
been Abbasid allies all along.

92
00:03:18,037 --> 00:03:19,965
We honestly don't know if it was

93
00:03:19,965 --> 00:03:20,447
planned.

94
00:03:20,667 --> 00:03:21,812
What do you think:

95
00:03:21,812 --> 00:03:23,953
betrayal, or a deal made before the

96
00:03:23,953 --> 00:03:24,401
battle?

97
00:03:24,621 --> 00:03:26,071
Write it in the comments.

98
00:03:26,541 --> 00:03:28,302
Caught between two enemies,

99
00:03:28,302 --> 00:03:29,677
the Tang army broke.

100
00:03:29,897 --> 00:03:33,117
Chinese sources say 20,000 to 30,000 men

101
00:03:33,117 --> 00:03:36,135
were killed and fewer than 2,000 made

102
00:03:36,135 --> 00:03:36,873
it home.

103
00:03:37,343 --> 00:03:40,976
Arab sources claim 50,000 dead and 20,000

104
00:03:40,976 --> 00:03:41,866
prisoners.

105
00:03:42,086 --> 00:03:44,646
Those Arab numbers are bigger than the

106
00:03:44,646 --> 00:03:46,983
whole army Chinese records describe,

107
00:03:46,983 --> 00:03:49,431
which tells you how much the sources

108
00:03:49,431 --> 00:03:50,043
disagree.

109
00:03:50,513 --> 00:03:52,305
Gao Xianzhi escaped.

110
00:03:52,525 --> 00:03:55,273
In the narrow mountain passes his officer

111
00:03:55,273 --> 00:03:57,348
Li Siye cut a way through the

112
00:03:57,348 --> 00:04:00,263
crowd of fleeing soldiers with the remaining

113
00:04:00,263 --> 00:04:00,824
cavalry.

114
00:04:01,044 --> 00:04:03,290
Gao lived, but he would never lead

115
00:04:03,290 --> 00:04:05,161
an army this far west again.

116
00:04:05,631 --> 00:04:07,769
One of the captured men was a

117
00:04:07,769 --> 00:04:09,791
young officer called Du Huan.

118
00:04:10,011 --> 00:04:12,728
He spent around ten years in the

119
00:04:12,728 --> 00:04:15,852
Abbasid world, was released in 762 and

120
00:04:15,852 --> 00:04:18,161
made his way back to China.

121
00:04:18,631 --> 00:04:20,640
He wrote about what he saw:

122
00:04:20,640 --> 00:04:24,009
Chinese silk weavers and painters already working

123
00:04:24,009 --> 00:04:25,900
in Abbasid cities, mosques,

124
00:04:25,900 --> 00:04:28,915
and customs the Chinese had never described

125
00:04:28,915 --> 00:04:29,447
before.

126
00:04:29,667 --> 00:04:32,202
Only fragments of his book survive,

127
00:04:32,202 --> 00:04:34,979
quoted in a later Chinese encyclopedia.

128
00:04:35,199 --> 00:04:37,380
It is one of the earliest Chinese

129
00:04:37,380 --> 00:04:39,935
eyewitness accounts of the Islamic world.

130
00:04:40,405 --> 00:04:41,386
Myth check.

131
00:04:41,606 --> 00:04:44,213
You may have read that Chinese prisoners

132
00:04:44,213 --> 00:04:46,928
from Talas taught papermaking in Samarkand,

133
00:04:46,928 --> 00:04:49,154
and that is how paper reached the

134
00:04:49,154 --> 00:04:51,163
Islamic world and later Europe.

135
00:04:51,383 --> 00:04:52,898
It's a great story.

136
00:04:53,368 --> 00:04:56,136
The problem: it first appears in a

137
00:04:56,136 --> 00:04:59,827
writer named al-Tha'alibi, about 250 years after

138
00:04:59,827 --> 00:05:00,749
the battle.

139
00:05:00,969 --> 00:05:03,892
No Chinese source and no contemporary Arab

140
00:05:03,892 --> 00:05:05,236
source mentions it.

141
00:05:05,456 --> 00:05:08,891
And paper was already known in Central

142
00:05:08,891 --> 00:05:10,832
Asia long before 751.

143
00:05:11,052 --> 00:05:12,333
Talas may have helped,

144
00:05:12,333 --> 00:05:14,231
but it probably wasn't the start.

145
00:05:14,701 --> 00:05:17,744
Many videos say Talas decided that Central

146
00:05:17,744 --> 00:05:20,909
Asia would become Muslim instead of Chinese.

147
00:05:21,129 --> 00:05:23,134
The truth is more interesting.

148
00:05:23,354 --> 00:05:24,784
Just one year later,

149
00:05:24,784 --> 00:05:27,301
Abbasid envoys were back in Chang'an.

150
00:05:27,771 --> 00:05:31,011
What really ended Chinese power in the

151
00:05:31,011 --> 00:05:33,335
west came four years later,

152
00:05:33,335 --> 00:05:36,083
in 755: the An Lushan rebellion,

153
00:05:36,083 --> 00:05:39,393
a civil war inside China so destructive

154
00:05:39,393 --> 00:05:42,704
that the Tang pulled their armies home.

155
00:05:42,924 --> 00:05:45,215
As historian James Millward put it,

156
00:05:45,215 --> 00:05:47,289
it was not the defeat at Talas

157
00:05:47,289 --> 00:05:49,580
that forced the Chinese to retreat.

158
00:05:50,050 --> 00:05:51,885
Look at the map then and now.

159
00:05:52,105 --> 00:05:55,206
In 751 Tang garrisons stood at the

160
00:05:55,206 --> 00:05:56,905
edge of Kyrgyzstan.

161
00:05:57,125 --> 00:06:00,246
It would take about a thousand years,

162
00:06:00,246 --> 00:06:03,853
until the Qing dynasty conquered Xinjiang in

163
00:06:03,853 --> 00:06:07,390
1759, before a Chinese state ruled Xinjiang

164
00:06:07,390 --> 00:06:09,541
again, just east of here.

165
00:06:10,011 --> 00:06:12,482
Today, the Talas valley is part of

166
00:06:12,482 --> 00:06:15,541
Kazakhstan and Kyrgyzstan, and the cities of

167
00:06:15,541 --> 00:06:18,130
Taraz and Talas stand near the place

168
00:06:18,130 --> 00:06:20,719
where two empires met once and never

169
00:06:20,719 --> 00:06:21,189
again.

170
00:06:21,659 --> 00:06:23,566
So who really won at Talas:

171
00:06:23,566 --> 00:06:26,538
the Abbasids, the Karluks who switched sides,

172
00:06:26,538 --> 00:06:29,061
or the rebellion that came four years

173
00:06:29,061 --> 00:06:29,510
later?

174
00:06:29,730 --> 00:06:31,181
Tell me in the comments.

175
00:06:31,401 --> 00:06:33,525
And if you want the story of

176
00:06:33,525 --> 00:06:36,417
what happened when the Mongols arrived in

177
00:06:36,417 --> 00:06:38,778
this same region 470 years later,

178
00:06:38,778 --> 00:06:41,257
subscribe and turn on notifications.

179
